# Γ₀(M) lies in the subgroup generated by T and q∣ b

Theorem statement: `CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma0_le_closure_T_union_setOf_dvd`
(`168c8591-27b0-566d-a1ab-9f5ebc191205`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `8c5228fe-c9a1-5798-981d-7911de1bcddf`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $M$ be a natural number and let $q$ be a non-zero natural number. Inside
$\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, consider the set consisting of the translation matrix
$T=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$ (Mathlib's `ModularGroup.T`) together with all
$\gamma\in\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ whose lower-left entry $\gamma_{1,0}$ is divisible by $M$ and
whose upper-right entry $\gamma_{0,1}$ is divisible by $q$, the divisibilities being taken in
$\mathbb{Z}$ with $M$ and $q$ cast from $\mathbb{N}$. The assertion is the inclusion of subgroups
$\Gamma_0(M)\le\langle\{T\}\cup\{\gamma: M\mid \gamma_{1,0},\ q\mid\gamma_{0,1}\}\rangle$, where
$\Gamma_0(M)$ is `CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma0 M`, the subgroup of matrices whose lower-left entry
vanishes in $\mathbb{Z}/M$, and the right-hand side is the subgroup generated by the displayed set.
No primality is assumed of $q$, and $M$ is an arbitrary natural number; only $q\neq 0$ is required.

A generation statement for $\Gamma_0(M)$: every element of $\Gamma_0(M)$ is a product of
translations and of elements of $\Gamma_0(M)$ whose upper-right entry is divisible by a prescribed
non-zero integer $q$. It is used in the proof of
[`CuspForm.eq_zero_of_prime_not_dvd_of_qCoeff_eq_zero`]
(thm.html#CuspForm.eq_zero_of_prime_not_dvd_of_qCoeff_eq_zero),
where a vanishing property invariant under translations need only be checked on matrices with $q\mid
b$.

